capability thread/start  a function run on a thread of its own, on one value
  heroes  threads.spawn
  go      WaitGroup.Go
  rust    thread::spawn, Builder::new, Builder::spawn, Builder::spawn_unchecked
  python  threading.Thread, Thread.start, Thread.run
  note    one `i64` in and one back, spec § 13; Go's `go` statement and Python's and Rust's closures carry anything, which here would be shared memory. A refusal of the system is `limit`, where Rust's Builder answers an error and the others raise or panic

capability thread/start-sized  a thread with a stack of a chosen size
  heroes  threads.spawn_sized
  go      -
  rust    Builder::stack_size
  python  threading.stack_size
  note    a least size in bytes for this thread alone, where Python's sets it for every later thread; 0 is the runtime's own floor, at least the main thread's

capability thread/wait  a thread waited for, its answer read
  heroes  threads.wait
  go      WaitGroup.Wait, WaitGroup.Add, WaitGroup.Done
  rust    JoinHandle::join, ScopedJoinHandle::join
  python  Thread.join, Future.result
  note    a thread is a value the program owes a wait: one never waited for stops the program when `main` returns, saying how many (observed), where Python and Go let it run on and Rust detaches it; waited for twice, itself or a copy, stops it at the second wait (observed). The count is the language's own count of C lives, so the place it names is inside the package

capability thread/text  a function run on a thread on one text, a copy each way
  heroes  threads.spawn_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `threads.wait_text` waits for it; its own record, `TextThread`, so a wait by the door of the other kind is a compile error rather than the runtime's panic. The runtime's text door answers no refusal, so a refusal of the system aborts

capability thread/run-many  a function run over a list of values on several threads, the answers in order
  heroes  threads.run_all
  go      -
  rust    thread::scope, Scope::spawn, Builder::spawn_scoped
  python  concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor, Executor.map, ThreadPoolExecutor.map, ThreadPoolExecutor.submit, ThreadPoolExecutor.shutdown, concurrent.futures.wait, concurrent.futures.ALL_COMPLETED
  note    the answers in the order of the values, as Python's `map` answers; at most `workers` at once, bounded by the runtime's ceiling so the call never meets its panic (observed: 300 values with 1,000 workers). Every thread is waited for before it answers, which is what Rust's scope promises

capability thread/run-many-text  a function run over a list of texts on several threads, the answers in order
  heroes  threads.run_all_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    texts of more than one byte and the empty text cross whole, observed

capability thread/ceiling  how many threads may run at once
  heroes  threads.max_running
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the runtime's own ceiling, the same on every platform; past it a start aborts, which Python and Go leave to the system

capability thread/processors  how many processors the program may run on, the usual number of workers
  heroes  os.processor_count
  go      -
  rust    thread::available_parallelism
  python  -
  note    the package `os` asks the system; Python's `os.cpu_count` stands in `os`'s own ledger

capability thread/sleep  the current thread made to wait a while
  heroes  time.sleep
  go      -
  rust    thread::sleep, thread::sleep_ms, thread::sleep_until, thread::park_timeout, thread::park_timeout_ms
  python  threading.Timer
  note    the package `time`; Python's Timer runs a function after a delay, which is a thread whose body sleeps first

capability thread/is-finished  whether a thread has ended, asked without waiting for it, or a wait bounded in time
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    JoinHandle::is_finished, ScopedJoinHandle::is_finished
  python  Thread.is_alive, Thread.join, Future.done, Future.running, concurrent.futures.as_completed, concurrent.futures.FIRST_COMPLETED, concurrent.futures.FIRST_EXCEPTION, threading.TIMEOUT_MAX
  note    the runtime's doors answer nothing about a thread but its wait, which blocks without a limit; it needs a door of the runtime that asks a thread's state, which this package cannot write. Answers in the order threads end, Python's `as_completed`, wait for the same door

capability thread/cancel  a running thread asked to stop
  heroes  refused: nothing crosses into a running thread, so nothing can ask it to stop; a body that must end early is given a bound in its value
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Future.cancel, Future.cancelled

capability thread/identity  which thread this is, its name or number, the threads running
  heroes  refused: a body is given one value and nothing else, so a thread that needs to know which it is is given that as its value; a started thread's number is its record's `handle`
  go      -
  rust    thread::current, thread::current_id, Thread::id, Thread::name, Thread::os_id, JoinHandle::thread, ScopedJoinHandle::thread, Builder::name, ThreadId
  python  threading.current_thread, threading.currentThread, threading.main_thread, threading.get_ident, threading.get_native_id, threading.enumerate, threading.active_count, threading.activeCount, Thread.name, Thread.ident, Thread.native_id, Thread.getName, Thread.setName

capability thread/detach  a thread the program need not wait for
  heroes  refused: every thread is waited for, which is how one forgotten is told; a thread left running past `main` would be cut off with its work half done
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Thread.daemon, Thread.isDaemon, Thread.setDaemon

capability thread/shared-state  locks, conditions, channels, once-only values and memory several threads reach
  heroes  refused: threads share nothing in Heroes, so no value is held by two threads and nothing needs a lock
  go      Mutex, Mutex.Lock, Mutex.TryLock, Mutex.Unlock, RWMutex, RWMutex.Lock, RWMutex.RLock, RWMutex.RLocker, RWMutex.RUnlock, RWMutex.TryLock, RWMutex.TryRLock, RWMutex.Unlock, Locker, Cond, NewCond, Cond.Broadcast, Cond.Signal, Cond.Wait, Once, Once.Do, OnceFunc, OnceValue, OnceValues, Map and its Clear, CompareAndDelete, CompareAndSwap, Delete, Load, LoadAndDelete, LoadOrStore, Range, Store and Swap, Pool, Pool.Get, Pool.Put
  rust    thread::park, Thread::unpark, thread::yield_now, LocalKey, AccessError
  python  threading.Lock, threading.RLock, threading.Condition, threading.Semaphore, threading.BoundedSemaphore, threading.Event, threading.Barrier, threading.BrokenBarrierError, threading.local, threading.WeakSet, threading.concurrent_tee, threading.serialize_iterator, threading.synchronized_iterator, threading.ThreadError

capability thread/failure  a failure inside a thread carried to the one that waits
  heroes  refused: an abort in a body ends the program, as everywhere in the language, and an error is a value the body writes into its answer; there is no exception to carry
  go      -
  rust    thread::panicking, thread::Result
  python  threading.excepthook, threading.ExceptHookArgs, Future.exception

dropped executor-plumbing  what a future and an executor need to be built by hand: here a pool is one call
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  concurrent.futures.Executor, concurrent.futures.Future, Future.add_done_callback, Future.set_result, Future.set_exception, Future.set_running_or_notify_cancel, ThreadPoolExecutor.prepare_context, ThreadPoolExecutor.BROKEN, concurrent.futures.BrokenExecutor, concurrent.futures.InvalidStateError, concurrent.futures.CancelledError, concurrent.futures.TimeoutError

dropped other-workers  workers that are processes or interpreters, the package `process`'s ground
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor, concurrent.futures.InterpreterPoolExecutor

dropped tracing  hooks into every thread's start and every call: a debugger's, not a program's
  go      -
  rust    thread::add_spawn_hook, Builder::no_hooks
  python  threading.settrace, threading.settrace_all_threads, threading.gettrace, threading.setprofile, threading.setprofile_all_threads, threading.getprofile

dropped raw-handles-and-traits  the system's own thread handle and what a trait gives every type: the handle here is the runtime's index, never the system's
  go      -
  rust    JoinHandle::as_handle, JoinHandle::as_pthread_t, JoinHandle::as_raw_handle, JoinHandle::into_pthread_t, JoinHandle::into_raw_handle, Thread::from_raw, Thread::into_raw, and on every type `fmt`, `borrow`, `borrow_mut`, `clone`, `clone_from`, `clone_into`, `clone_to_uninit`, `from`, `into`, `to_owned`, `try_from`, `try_into`, `type_id`
  python  -
